def sort_merge_join(left, right):
    """left and right are lists of (key, value) pairs.
    Return the sorted list of (key, left_value, right_value) for every pair of rows with equal keys."""
    left, right = sorted(left), sorted(right)
    out, i, j = [], 0, 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i][0] < right[j][0]:
            i += 1
        elif left[i][0] > right[j][0]:
            j += 1
        else:
            key = left[i][0]
            i_end, j_end = i, j
            while i_end < len(left) and left[i_end][0] == key:
                i_end += 1
            while j_end < len(right) and right[j_end][0] == key:
                j_end += 1
            for a in range(i, i_end):
                for b in range(j, j_end):
                    out.append((key, left[a][1], right[b][1]))
            i, j = i_end, j_end
    return sorted(out)

exercises.solution:
The merge walks each sorted list once; only runs of equal keys produce a product:

```python
def sort_merge_join(left, right):
    left, right = sorted(left), sorted(right)
    out, i, j = [], 0, 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i][0] < right[j][0]:
            i += 1
        elif left[i][0] > right[j][0]:
            j += 1
        else:
            key = left[i][0]
            i_end, j_end = i, j
            while i_end < len(left) and left[i_end][0] == key:
                i_end += 1
            while j_end < len(right) and right[j_end][0] == key:
                j_end += 1
            for a in range(i, i_end):
                for b in range(j, j_end):
                    out.append((key, left[a][1], right[b][1]))
            i, j = i_end, j_end
    return sorted(out)
```

Cost: $O(|L| \log |L| + |R| \log |R|)$ for the sorts, plus time linear in the input and output for the merge. A common bug is to advance only one index after a match, which misses the other combinations of duplicate keys; another is to forget that the output of a join with many duplicates can be much larger than either input (a key appearing 1,000 times on each side produces a million rows).

